         room='"Which constraint is real, which excuse is convenient, and what responsibility remains?"',
         explain=('In 60 words: what is Sartre\'s bad faith?',
                  'Bad faith is self-deception about freedom. Someone treats a role, job title or social script as a fixed essence, or pretends that freedom means having no constraints. Sartre\'s point is not that circumstances are unreal. It is that we still interpret and answer them, so saying the role made the choice can hide responsibility.'),
         recall=[('How is absurdism different from existentialism?', 'Camus\'s absurdism names the clash between our demand for meaning and an indifferent world, then recommends lucid revolt. Existentialism is a broader, diverse family about existence, freedom, responsibility and meaning.'),
                 ('What is bad faith?', 'A way of hiding one\'s situated freedom by treating a role, excuse or circumstance as a fixed essence that removes responsibility.'),
                 ('What is the honest claim about Frankl\'s camps?', 'He was imprisoned in four camps from 1942 to 1945 and later interpreted survival through meaning. His prewar logotherapy predates the camps, and his story cannot be turned into a universal promise that meaning defeats oppression.')],
